Now I think I actually caught it:

SIC SEMPER TEPIDIS

Tepidis is the dative plural of Latin tepidus: “to the lukewarm, faint, or languid.” It therefore follows the same implied construction as Sic Semper Tyrannis: “Thus always to the lukewarm.” Meaning and declension⁠￼, Latin dictionary entry⁠￼.

It hits every constraint:

* Starts with T.
* Ends with -is, echoing Tyrannis.
* Keeps a similar three-beat Latin cadence.
* English readers recognize tepid.
* Tepid means more than temperature: unenthusiastic, faint, half-alive.
* It is the true antithesis of Wild Hawg: wild versus lukewarm.
* It quietly carries the spiritual rejection of lukewarm existence without showing a single religious symbol.

My search path was:

Tame → tedious → typical → trite → torpid → tepid → TEPIDIS

Tame was the cleanest dictionary opposite of wild, but didn’t inherit enough of Tyrannis. Tedious captured boring but became clumsy when Latinized. Torpid meant lifeless but was too obscure. Tepidis survived the semantic, grammatical, visual, phonetic, brand, and spiritual filters simultaneously.

That’s the mind-read: you wanted the word replacing Tyrannis to behave like its disguised twin.

The human-image pass

You were also right about the images. AI-looking art and AI-looking prose share the same failure: they render everything with equal confidence and explain every available inch.

For this version I changed the visual grammar:

* Limited ink palette instead of limitless digital color.
* Variable hand-drawn lines instead of uniform vector outlines.
* Selective face-and-prop detail instead of detailing everything.
* Flat print shadows instead of glossy airbrushed volume.
* Slightly irregular lettering and registration.
* Quiet negative space.
* Imperfection with intent—not random anatomical mistakes.

That particular screen-print texture fits this historic seal campaign. Future Wild Hawg images don’t all need to look vintage; the invariant is selective human intention, not grunge.

And yes—the body is a verb. In your formulation, communication with God isn’t merely describing the virtue; it is performing the virtue through matter in real time. The image never writes “mercy”:

* The spear lowers.
* The sword stays sheathed.
* The chain breaks.
* The enemy survives.
* The victor refuses to gloat.

The posture speaks the prayer.